course: dictionary
lesson: 8545
title: Scald and the words around it
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: A scald is a burn caused by very hot liquid or steam, and it is a common injury for young children in the kitchen and bathroom. Scald can be a verb (you can scald your hand) or a noun (a scald on your arm). Look at the words around it to work out which.
explain: «A girl in Leeds left a pan handle sticking out over the edge of the cooker; her little brother pulled it and was scalded on his arm.» The pan was hot, and the boy was hurt on his arm, so scalded must mean burned by something hot. The clues in the sentence did the work for you.
explain: Scald has a cooking meaning too. To scald milk is to heat it until it is almost boiling. Check by swapping in your meaning: a recipe that says «scald the milk» wants you to heat it nearly to boiling, not to burn your skin.
fact: To scald means to burn your skin with very hot liquid or steam.
fact: A scald is an injury to the skin caused by hot liquid or steam.
fact: To scald milk means to heat it until it is almost boiling.
fact: «Scalding hot» means so hot that it could burn your skin.
fact: Steam can scald you even when you cannot see it, because the white cloud is made of tiny droplets as steam cools.
exercise: «A hot drink can still scald a small child badly fifteen minutes after it was made.» What does scald mean here? | To make a drink sweet | To cool a drink down slowly | To wash a cup | *To burn the skin with very hot liquid or steam
exercise: A recipe says «scald the milk». What should you do to the milk? | *Heat it until it is almost boiling | Freeze it until it is solid | Add sugar to it and stir | Pour it down the sink
exercise: What is a scald? | *An injury to the skin caused by hot liquid or steam | A bath toy | A kind of kettle | A cold drink spilled on the floor
test: In «scalding hot», what does scalding mean? | A little bit cool | *So hot it could burn your skin | Wet and slippery | Very cold, like ice
test: Why does the girl in Sydney's adult test the bath water with an elbow? | Because it makes the water cooler | To check how deep the water is | *Because an elbow feels heat better than a hand, so hot water is less likely to scald | Because elbows are always clean
exam: Which first aid step is right for a scald? | *Cool it under cool, not icy, running water for 20 minutes and tell an adult | Pop it straight in the freezer | Put butter on it and wrap it tightly | Rub it hard with a towel
